  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nToday into Tonight - Breezy with near-record or record warmth\n\nA deepening low pressure system will move northeast of the\nCanadian Prairies through tonight. The track of this low is a \nbit further northwest as a result, the stronger 850 mb winds are\na bit lower than what they looked like on Friday. South wind \ngusts will still range from 25 to 35 mph with the highest over \nsoutheast Minnesota0 (40-80 percent in the 10.00z HREF) for 35 \nmph or higher gusts. The probability of wind gust of 45 mph or \ngreater have dropped to 0 percent and the probability of 40 mph \nor greater is only up to 15 percent for today. There is an \ninteresting increase in the 10.00z HREF wind gusts probabilities\nof over 45 mph (30-60%) in southeast Minnesota between 9 PM and\n3 AM tonight. If this trend continues, we might need a Wind \nAdvisory for this time period.\n\nSouthwest winds aloft will bring unusually warm 850 mb\ntemperatures (18 to 21C) northward into the region. With\ntemperatures anomalies anywhere from 2 to 2.5 standard \ndeviation above-normal, high temperatures will be near-record to\nrecord in the lower and mid-80s. With breezy southwest winds\ntonight, low temperatures will only fall into the lower and\nmid-60s. Due to this, there could be some warm low temperatures\nfor Sunday.\n\nSunday into Sunday night - More record warmth possible\n\nLike Saturday, 850 mb temperatures will range from 18 to 21C.\nThese temperatures are once again 2 to 2.5 standard deviations\nabove-normal. With high temperatures in the lower and mid-80s,\ntemperatures will once again be near-record or record. The\nwell-above normal warmth will continue with Sunday night with\nlow temperatures in the mid- to upper 60s.\n\nMonday Afternoon into Monday Night - Small rain chances\n\nA cold front will move through the region on Monday night. Like\nthe past couple of days, there is some concern on how saturated\nit will become below 700 mb along and south of Interstate 94\nare only 20-30 percent. Meanwhile, there looks to be much \ndeeper saturation north of Interstate 94. This results in much \nhigher precipitation chances of 30-70 percent in this area.\n\nWith the front arriving a bit later in the day, high\ntemperatures were increased and they now range from the mid-70s\nto lower 80s.\n\nTuesday through Friday\n\nThere might be some precipitation from Thursday into Friday as \na trough moves through the region, but confidence on the timing \nand even how far south it will extend are highly uncertain.\n\nTemperatures will be more seasonable with high temperatures in\nthe 50s and 60s and low temperatures ranging from the mid-30s to\nlower 50s.",
